  "result": "The 30-minute lesson on objective O2 is written, with a five-item quiz and its key. I didn't get a word count: the command was blocked. By my estimate the lesson is about 3,900 words, inside the 2,700–6,750 range that counts as fitting.\n\n- **Lesson:** `lessons/0001-plant-foot-striking-surface-and-lean.html`. It opens with a penalty that went over the bar, which matches your \"plant too far behind\" habit.\n  - It covers the plant foot's effect on aim and height, the laces versus the inside of the foot, and how the lean affects height.\n  - It has two diagrams, three quick checks, a worked example, a practice task, a summary and sources.\n- **Quiz:** `quizzes/0002.json` and `keys/0002.json`. It has five items and none repeat the lesson's practice questions.\n- **Other files:** `RESOURCES.md` is new, and `NOTES.md` now records the misconceptions M1–M4 and where you are.\n\nThe research is thin on this topic. I found no study that tests plant-foot distance or trunk lean on penalty height. The link from plant-foot position and lean to height rests on coaching consensus plus the physics of where the foot meets the ball, and the lesson says so. The laces-for-power and inside-foot-for-accuracy trade-off is better supported. I only read the abstract and a summary of the Kellis & Katis review, and I didn't open the other cited papers, so the lesson leans on search-result summaries for them.\n\nOne thing to try on the pitch: plant beside the ball with your chest over it, and change only that for ten kicks.\n\nQuiz 0002 is ready whenever you want to take it, and you can ask me anything about the lesson first.",
